00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:37.770
This session will walk through,
in detail, the queries,

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.110
the code, and the
configurations used

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.350
to train a model on stock
market tick data sourced

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.610
from Thomson Reuters.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.590
Specifically, the
aim of this model

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:49.140
was to predict returns
on a five minute

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.450
horizon of market information.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.940
Now, this project
is somewhat special.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.000
And that's because it's really
doing everything in the cloud.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.300
The data is in the cloud.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.360
The preprocessing is
done in the cloud.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.490
And the training of the
machine learning model--

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:03.987
well, it's also
done in the cloud.

00:01:03.987 --> 00:01:06.570
This means that even though the
particular model they ended up

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:09.420
using was pretty simple, and
the training data selected

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.280
was over a fairly
limited frame, just

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.380
five minutes, the solution
that was designed--

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.830
it works from the
get go to scale up

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:21.100
to a much bigger data set and a
much more sophisticated model.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.670
Now, concretely, the data
is pulled out of BigQuery

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.860
with a couple of
different SQL queries.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.560
And then it's loaded
into a pandas DataFrame.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.830
From there, it's transformed
into TFRecord files.

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:35.140
This is ideal for sending
over to TensorFlow training,

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.980
especially if the data were to
then scale up in the future.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:39.960
There's a great
explanation in the talk

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.990
of using feature columns and the
feature spec utility functions

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:46.890
to make constructing an input
function much, much easier.

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:49.910
And, as is often the case, the
simplest part of the process

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:53.340
ended up being the
actual model building.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.380
Now for the training
of this model,

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.000
the model was trained
on Cloud ML Engine,

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.390
and there is a detailed walk
through of the configuration

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.910
file and setup
process that was used,

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:05.730
empowering you to not
only be able to reproduce

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:09.180
this workflow, but
then extend it.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.050
This session was very
much a how to talk.

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.930
So if you're ready to dive into
the world of running machine

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.960
learning in the cloud, head
on over to the full talk

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:20.160
and get training.

